course: biology
lesson: 74
title: Fossils: how we know about life long ago
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A fossil is a message from a world nobody saw: the remains or traces of a living thing kept in rock, ice or amber for thousands or millions of years.
explain: Most fossils form when an animal is buried fast in mud; over ages the mud becomes rock and minerals slowly replace the bone or shell, leaving a stone copy like the spiral ammonite you might find on the beach at Lyme Regis.
explain: Fossils are not only bones: footprints, burrows, tooth marks and even fossil poo tell scientists how animals walked, what they ate and who bit whom.
explain: In films dinosaurs are cloned from blood in amber mosquitoes, and in cartoons cavemen ride dinosaurs; real fossils say no to both, because DNA breaks down far too fast and tens of millions of years separate dinosaurs from people.
fact: Ammonites were sea animals with coiled shells, related to octopuses and squid, that died out with the dinosaurs about 66 million years ago; fossil hunters keep away from cliff bottoms, where rocks fall, and check the tide times.
fact: Mary Anning of Lyme Regis found, at about 12 with her brother, the first full ichthyosaur skeleton recognised by scientists around 1811, and later the first complete plesiosaur; women were not allowed into scientific societies, so men often got the credit.
fact: Most fossils form when a living thing is buried quickly, and minerals carried by water fill or replace the remains; only a tiny fraction of living things ever become fossils, because most remains are scattered, eaten or rot first, and soft animals like jellyfish, with no hard parts, almost never do.
fact: Trace fossils such as footprints, burrows, nests and tooth marks are signs of life, and the distance between steps tells how fast a dinosaur walked.
fact: Amber is fossilised tree resin that can trap small insects; dinosaurs cannot be cloned from amber insects because DNA breaks down far too fast to last 66 million years.
fact: Rock layers are laid one on top of another, so lower layers are usually older; scientists date volcanic ash layers with natural atomic clocks in the rock.
fact: Megalodon was a huge shark from about 23 to 3.6 million years ago with teeth up to about 18 centimetres; its skeleton was cartilage, which rarely fossilises, so mostly teeth remain.
fact: Coprolites are fossilised droppings that show what ancient animals ate; William Buckland recognised them in the 1820s, and Mary Anning noticed them inside ichthyosaur skeletons.
fact: Lyuba, a baby woolly mammoth about 42,000 years old, was found in 2007 in frozen Russian ground with her skin and trunk; mammoths survived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago, after the Egyptian pyramids were built about 4,500 years ago.
fact: Archaeopteryx lived about 150 million years ago in what is now Germany, with feathers and wings like a bird but teeth, claws and a bony tail; it was found in 1861.
fact: Rocks near the top of Mount Everest hold fossils of sea creatures, because India pushing into Asia lifted an old sea floor into the Himalayas, which still rise a few millimetres a year.
fact: Sue, one of the largest and most complete T. rex skeletons with about 90 percent of the bones, was found in South Dakota in 1990 by Sue Hendrickson and stands in the Field Museum in Chicago; the skeleton is named after its finder, and nobody knows if Sue was female or male.
exercise: What were ammonites? | Sea animals with coiled shells, related to octopuses and squid | Giant snails with coiled shells that crawled about in hot sandy deserts | Stone flowers that grew on the sea floor | Baby dinosaurs curled up in their eggs
exercise: When did the ammonites die out? | About 66 million years ago, with the dinosaurs | About 66 years ago, in one very cold winter | About 6,000 years ago, in a great flood | They never died out and still live today
exercise: What is the safest way to hunt fossils on a beach? | Stay away from the cliff bottom and check the tide times | Climb the cliff to reach the best layers, as high as you can get | Dig into the bottom of the cliff with a spade | Go out at high tide when rocks are covered
exercise: What did Mary Anning find with her brother at about 12 years old? | The first full ichthyosaur skeleton known to science | The first T. rex skull ever dug up, in a cliff near her home | A frozen woolly mammoth with its hair and skin still on | The very first Archaeopteryx feather ever found in stone
exercise: Why did men often get the credit for Mary Anning's finds? | Women were not allowed to join scientific societies | She never told anyone at all what she had found on the beach | Her finds were all fakes | She sold her fossils only to children
exercise: How do most fossils form? | Remains are buried fast and minerals slowly replace them | Animals turn to solid stone the very moment that they die | Bones are carved into stone by rivers | Lightning strikes bones and hardens them
exercise: Why are jellyfish so rarely found as fossils? | They have no hard parts and usually rot before burial | Jellyfish only appeared in the sea a few hundred years ago | Jellyfish fossils dissolve in sea water | Scientists never look for them
exercise: What is a trace fossil? | A sign of life, like a footprint, burrow or tooth mark | A tiny piece that has broken off a much bigger fossil bone | A drawing of an animal made by early people | A fossil found by following a map
exercise: What can the gap between dinosaur footprints tell scientists? | How fast the dinosaur was walking | What colour the dinosaur's skin was | How old the dinosaur was, in years | What sounds the dinosaur made to its herd
exercise: What is amber? | Fossilised tree resin | Melted dinosaur bone gone clear | Hardened honey from ancient bees | Orange glass made by volcanoes
exercise: In a cliff of rock layers, which layer is usually oldest? | The lowest one | The highest one | The middle one of the three | The thickest one
exercise: Why do we find megalodon teeth but almost no megalodon skeletons? | Its skeleton was cartilage, which rarely fossilises | Megalodon had no skeleton at all, only teeth and soft flesh | Its bones were all eaten by whales | Its skeletons are too heavy to dig up
exercise: What are coprolites? | Fossilised droppings | Fossilised dinosaur eggs | Fossilised footprints | Fossilised bird feathers
exercise: Why was the baby mammoth Lyuba so well preserved? | She lay in frozen ground that worked like a giant freezer | She was turned to stone in the hot ash of an erupting volcano | She was trapped in a huge piece of amber | She was buried in salt by the sea
exercise: Which features made Archaeopteryx a creature in between two groups? | Feathers and wings like a bird with teeth and a bony tail | Fins like a fish with legs like a frog | Fur like a mammal with scales like a snake on its back and tail | A shell like a turtle with wings like a bat
exercise: Why are there sea creature fossils near the top of Mount Everest? | India pushing into Asia lifted an old sea floor into mountains | Seabirds carried the shells up and dropped them | The sea once covered the whole world right up to the very top of every mountain | Climbers left them there as a joke
exercise: What is special about the T. rex called Sue? | It is one of the largest and most complete, with about 90% of its bones | It is the only T. rex ever found anywhere | It was found alive in a South Dakota cave | It is proven to be a female, because of the name the scientists gave it
test: «saw in a film you can clone dinos from a mosquito in amber, so when's the zoo opening» What is right? | Never, DNA breaks down far too fast to last 66 million years | Soon, scientists already cloned one in 2010 | Yes, but only the small dinosaurs can be cloned, not the giants | Yes, any amber older than a year has dinosaur blood
test: In a cartoon, cavemen ride a dinosaur to work. What do fossils tell us? | Tens of millions of years separate the last dinosaurs and the first humans | People and dinosaurs lived together for a short time | Cavemen hunted dinosaurs with spears | Dinosaurs died out only a few thousand years ago
test: «found a shell fossil on a mountain, so the flood must've covered the mountains» How could a scientist test other ideas? | Look for evidence that the rocks formed on a sea floor and rose | Assume the oldest story is the right one | Decide it was dropped by a bird and stop looking | Count the shells and multiply the number by the height of the mountain
test: «just gonna climb up the cliff, best fossils are up top» What is the best answer? | No, rockfalls happen there; search the beach away from the cliff | Fine, cliffs are solid rock and never fall | Fine, as long as the tide is in | Fine, just dig into the cliff bottom with a spade instead, it's quicker
test: What would happen to a dead fish lying on a dry beach for a week? | It would rot or be eaten, so it would almost never become a fossil | It would turn into solid stone within a few days in the hot sun | It would certainly become a perfect fossil | It would dissolve in the sand and leave a bone
test: «that shiny brown stone's just a rock, why's it in a museum» What could it be? | A coprolite, fossil poo that shows what an animal ate | A dinosaur egg that never hatched | A piece of melted meteor that fell from space onto the beach | A stone a dinosaur swallowed for fun
test: «Sue the T. rex is a girl, the name says so» What is right? | The name comes from her finder; nobody knows if Sue was female | Yes, all T. rex in museums are girls | Yes, the skeleton has a label inside the skull that says she was a girl | No, Sue is definitely a boy
test: A fossil lies in a low rock layer and another in a high layer of the same cliff. Which is probably older? | The one in the lower layer | The one in the higher layer | They must be exactly the same age | You can never tell anything from layers
test: «mammoths died out way before the pyramids, obvs» What is right? | Mammoths still lived on Wrangel Island when the pyramids were built | Yes, mammoths died out at the same time as the dinosaurs did | Yes, mammoths died out a million years ago | No, herds of mammoths still roam wild across Siberia today
test: «found a huge shark tooth on the beach, so there's a giant shark here now» What is right? | Sharks shed thousands of teeth, and many beach teeth are fossils | Yes, shark teeth only ever wash up when a shark is swimming close by | Yes, it must be a living megalodon | No, shark teeth are always fake
test: Why is a nearly complete skeleton like Sue so rare? | Most remains are scattered, eaten or rot before they are buried | Dinosaurs usually had very few bones | Museums throw away all the skeletons that are not complete | Most T. rex were buried twice
exam: Identify the principal process by which the majority of fossils are formed. | Rapid burial of remains followed by gradual replacement by minerals | Instantaneous petrification of the organism at the moment of death | The carving of bones into stone by the action of rivers | The hardening of bones by repeated lightning strikes during storms
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the contribution of Mary Anning. | She discovered the first complete ichthyosaur and plesiosaur skeletons known to science | She discovered the first skeleton of Tyrannosaurus rex in North America | She founded the first scientific society to admit women | She described the frozen mammoth Lyuba in Siberia
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relative age of strata in an undisturbed sequence of rock? | Lower strata are ordinarily older than those situated above them | Higher strata are ordinarily older than those situated below them | All strata in a given cliff are invariably of identical age | The thickness of a stratum alone determines its age
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the cloning of dinosaurs from insects preserved in amber is not possible. | Genetic material degrades far too rapidly to persist for such a period | Amber is too hard to be cut by any laboratory instruments presently available | Insects preserved in amber never fed upon dinosaurs | The cloning of any animal is prohibited by the laws of physics
